.TH NETHERNET-CLIENT 1 "November 2026" "bedrocknode-tunnel" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nethernet-client \- Bedrock Node Tunnel client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nethernet-client
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Connects a local Minecraft Bedrock Dedicated Server to the Bedrock Node Tunnel
relay so that players can join without port forwarding.
On the first run the client prints a claim link, waits until the tunnel is
claimed in the web dashboard, and saves the resulting token for later runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-token " TOKEN"
Tunnel token. When omitted, the saved token is used, or a new tunnel is claimed.
.TP
.B \-reset\-token
Delete the saved token and claim a new tunnel.
.TP
.BI \-control " HOST:PORT"
Relay control address.
.TP
.BI \-web " URL"
Web dashboard URL used for claiming a tunnel.
.TP
.BI \-local\-http " HOST:PORT"
Local Bedrock TCP target (default 127.0.0.1:19132).
.TP
.BI \-local\-udp\-ip " IP"
Local IP used for Bedrock UDP traffic. Detected automatically when empty.
.TP
.BI \-local\-udp\-port " PORT"
Fallback local UDP port (default 47334).
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bedrocknode-tunnel/bedrocknode-token.txt
Saved tunnel token.
.SH SERVICE
A systemd user unit is installed. Claim a tunnel once by running
.B nethernet-client
in a terminal, then run
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now bedrocknode-tunnel
to keep it running in the background.
